  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 243 PM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nUpper-level ridging continues over the southern CONUS with a \nstationary surface boundary draping across southern Iowa and the \nHigh Plains. Overnight showers and storms that developed along this \nboundary as the low-level jet ramped up were carried to the \nsoutheast, resulting in areas of light rain and widespread cloud \ncover continuing after sunrise this morning. Cloud cover may once \nagain mitigate some of the heat today, primarily north of the I-70 \ncorridor. This afternoon, a shortwave is expected to roll across \nnorthern Missouri. Confidence in adequate moisture advection into \nthe area is lower, however, so thinking a rather low-end probability \nof seeing any shower or storm during this timeframe. Even so, this \ncombined with the cloud cover possibility, was not confident in \nissuing a heat product north of the existing Extreme Heat Warning \ntoday. Expecting peak heat indices in the 105-110F range in the \nExtreme Heat Warning area with heat indices around 100-105F possible \nfurther north. \n\nLittle changes to the synoptic pattern are expected over the coming \ndays with the upper-level ridge remaining while several shortwaves \ntraverse the mean flow. Moreover, the surface boundary looks to take \nthe \"stationary\" part of its name to heart with only slight bobs to \nthe north and south through midweek. The corridor surrounding this \nboundary will be one to watch over the coming evenings with nearing \nshortwaves and the nightly uptick of the low-level jet. Generally \nspeaking, models have struggled to latch on to overnight and early \nmorning shower/storm potential as during earlier forecasts. We have \nseen this film before, though, and rather recently at that. In fact, \nthe next several overnight/early morning periods appear to be reruns \nof the past two mornings where shower/storm development was poorly \nresolved by CAMs but occurred nonetheless. Consequently, opted to \nonce again include 10-30% PoPs in our north during the early morning \nhours through midweek...should CAMs better resolve this potential, \nwould consider increasing PoPs in the north as well as possibly \nbringing some PoPs closer to the KC metro. A marginal (level 1 of 5) \nsevere risk will also exist with elevated overnight storms in the \nform of damaging wind gusts. Due to potential convective debris \nbleeding into the the solar heating period, no additional heat \nheadlines were issued this forecast cycle, and the issuance of such \nproducts will continue to be taken day-by-day (or forecast cycle by \nforecast cycle, if you will). That said, even though confidence in \nreaching Heat Advisory criteria is low, it will still be hot and \nvulnerable populations should continue to exercise heat precautions. \n\nThe stationary boundary will eventually crawl south as cold front, \nmost likely sometime between late Wednesday afternoon and early \nThursday morning. The passage of this front could reduce daily high \ntemperatures by as much as 5-10F along and north of the US-36 \ncorridor. Further south, it may not be until the weekend before \nthere is some reprieve from the heat. More rain chances arrive as \nthe front moves southward as well, although the US-36 corridor and \nnorth look to be the main beneficiaries.",
